# Ermuntre dich, mein schwacher Geist, BWV 454 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Sacred song No. 16 in the Mutopia edition (Franz Wullner's 1893
# edition of Schemelli's Musicalisches Gesang-Buch, Leipzig 1736), a
# Christmas song ("rouse yourself, my feeble spirit, to receive the little
# child"). Schemelli prints melody and figured bass; the bass here is the
# printed one, the figures are not played. This 3/4 melody is not
# Johann Schop's 4/4 chorale tune for the same text, the one Bach
# harmonized in the Christmas Oratorio ("Brich an, o schoenes
# Morgenlicht").
#
# Character: a gentle 3/4 song, mostly quarter and half notes, the bass
# walking under it with a few eighths. Warm, quietly joyful. Fermatas mark
# the line ends at bars 4, 16 and 19.
#
# Time signature: 3/4, 23 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI. The
# first section (bars 1-8) is repeated, carrying the first two lines of
# the text; the second section (9-23) is not. The MIDI does not play the
# repeat; it is written out with `play`. The first note is on the
# downbeat. Bar 16 is three beats: a half note with a fermata and the
# quarter upbeat to bar 17.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 76 (dotted half about 25). One stanza is 31 bars, 93
# quarters, as performed. MusicBrainz track times: Klaus Mertens,
# Christoph Pregardien, Ton Koopman and Jaap ter Linden 1:41; Hilde
# Roessel-Majdan 2:03; Sibylla Rubens, Michael Behringer and others
# 2:19. I could not find how many stanzas each recording sings, so these
# do not convert to a tempo with any confidence (one stanza in 1:41 would
# be about 55 with the fermatas; two would be about 110). 76 is my
# judgment: a flowing three-in-a-bar for a lullaby-like Christmas song,
# slow enough for the half notes to sing on the chip.
#
# Structure (Mutopia score and figures):
#   1-4    F major, line 1; half close on C with a fermata (bar 4)
#   5-8    line 2, cadence in F (bar 8)
#   9-12   second section: to G minor, cadence there (bar 12)
#   13-16  to D minor, cadence with a fermata (bar 16)
#   17-19  the high F (bar 17) and a half close on C with a fermata (19)
#   20-23  the last line, cadence in F
#
# Bars as performed (after `play`):
#   section 1 bars 1-8, its repeat 9-16 (score bar x is x+8), section 2
#   bars 17-31 (score bar 9+y is 17+y).
#
# Plan: the first lines mf, their repeat (new words) mp. The second
# section mf, softer for the turn to D minor, full at the high F, and
# settling for the last line. Fermata bars broadened.

tempo 76
play 1-8 1-23

voice mid 0        # two voices only: no middle voice to set back

dyn 1 mf           # bars 1-8: lines 1-2
dyn 9 mp           # bars 9-16: the repeat, lines 3-4
dyn 17 mf          # score 9-12: to G minor
dyn 21 mp          # score 13-16: the turn to D minor
dyn 25 f           # score 17-19: the high F
dyn 28 mf          # score 20-23: the last line

broaden 4 0.8      # fermata on C
broaden 8 0.92     # cadence in F, end of the section
broaden 12 0.8     # the same in the repeat
broaden 16 0.92
broaden 20 0.92    # score bar 12: cadence in G minor
broaden 24 0.78    # score bar 16: fermata, D minor, and the upbeat after
broaden 27 0.8     # score bar 19: fermata on C (beat 3)

rit 2 0.7          # score bars 22-23: the final cadence in F
